    /// selection. **No undo step, and no explaining**: a caller told about a
    /// rule here would go looking for one it did not break.
    ///
    /// **Not every "refuses" in this crate's doc comments reaches a caller
    /// as [`Outcome::Refused`].** A doc comment saying an op "refuses" is
    /// describing the DOMAIN, which is what a reader needs; this is the one
    /// place that says which of them can say so out loud.
    ///
    /// The rules that speak, after road map 7's D2·1 and D2·3 and spec §11
    /// #88: the overlap rule (`transition.rs`, the sentence every placement
    /// op hands on), a locked track (`edit_time.rs`'s `locked_track`, at
    /// `close_gap`, `insert_clip` and a rippled transition), the last track
    /// and a track that still holds clips, a media row a clip still shows
    /// (`edit_track.rs`), a cut with nothing left to give a transition, a
    /// request too short to make one and the two ways a rippled one has
    /// nowhere to shift to (`edit_transition.rs`), an action that would
    /// overlap another or run past its clip (`edit_action.rs`), a move to a
    /// track index the document has not got (`edit_clip.rs`), and the
    /// non-edits #88 gave a sentence: a roll with no cut (`edit_edge.rs`),
    /// `detach_audio` with no audio to detach, `set_transition_duration`
    /// with no clip before it or past the pair's overlap limit, and
    /// `close_gap` aimed at a clip or at a track with nothing after `at`.
    ///
    /// **What still answers `Identity` although a doc comment calls it a
    /// refusal**, deliberately (§11 #88): the clamped edge ops in
    /// `edit_edge.rs` and `edit_clip.rs` — a slip or a roll the clamps
    /// reduce to nothing, a trim whose two clamps crossed, a rate stretch
    /// outside `SPEED_MIN`–`SPEED_MAX` — each a clamp that landed on where the clip
    /// already was, which is the case where an identity is also the honest
    /// answer (kpgalligan/framejob#8, decision 3); and the unreachable misses
    /// — a clip absent from its own track's list in `add_transition` and
    /// `set_transition_duration`, and `close_gap`'s `end <= start` guard.
    ///
    /// The **empty-selection** answers are not on that list at all: an op
    /// handed an empty list of clips is a TRUE identity and stays one,
    /// deliberately (the same spec, decision 4). Counted at road map 7's
    /// D2·5 the family is FOURTEEN ops, eight of them behind a guard —
    /// `edit_clip`'s `move_clips`, `delete_clips` and `duplicate_clips`,
    /// `edit_props`'s `patch_clips` (the one guard behind all five patch
    /// ops), `edit_nest`'s `nest_clips`, `edit_paste`'s `paste` (an empty
    /// payload), and `edit_time`'s `ripple_delete` and
    /// `ripple_delete_ranges` (ranges that merge to nothing). Nothing was
    /// ASKED for at any of them, so there is no rule a sentence could name.
    /// The other two, `split_clips` and `paste_attributes`, reach the same
    /// answer with no guard at all, through `finish`.
    Identity,

    /// A rule declined the op, in the rule's own words. **No undo step**,
    /// exactly as for an identity — a refusal is not an error tier and the
    /// project is left exactly as it was — but the caller is told why.
    ///
    /// The sentence is the one the rule itself writes, passed through
    /// verbatim so it is written once: "clips 3 and 4 would overlap by 8000
    /// ticks, more than half of the shorter clip (6000 ticks)". It names
    /// what a caller could act on, and it is prose, not a code.
    ///
    /// **Every rule's sentence names the value, not the rule.** Which track
    /// is locked, which clips still show the media, how many frames the cut
    /// has left, which action is in the way: a caller that reads one knows
    /// what to send instead without asking the project again. Where an
    /// argument would let the op through, the sentence names that argument
    /// (`with_clips: true`, `ripple: true`) — advice a caller can act on,
    /// not a second description of the rule. A rule with more than one site
    /// writes its sentence once and the sites pass a clause
    /// (`edit_time::locked_track`, `edit_action`'s two, `edit_track`'s
    /// `clip_list`), so two ops cannot drift apart on the same rule.
    Refused(String),
}
