== transcribe
  desc: Transcribes clips and lays the result out as TEXT CLIPS, one per cue, on a new track of its own — File ▸ Generate Captions…. They are ordinary text clips in the Caption preset, exactly what import_captions makes, so they render in the preview, in render_frame and in an export (burn-in) and can be retimed with move_clip or restyled with set_text. Cues are at most two lines of 42 characters and at most 6 s, with a frame between them. A word caption STYLE (every style but subtitles) makes PAGES instead: each page is filled to the width it will MEASURE on this canvas in that style — at most 0.86 of the canvas width — not to a word count, on one line (karaoke: up to two); a page also closes at a change of speaker, a gap of 0.25 s or more, a word ending . ? ! or …, 3 s on screen, and one_word's single word; a lone word too wide shrinks to 80% before it wraps. Pages stay within their own clip and abut the next page where the gap is under 0.25 s, else hold 0.5 s after the last word. Each placed page is a text clip in the style's face, case and word_style carrying its words' timings, so the word being said is drawn as the style says. The result carries the cues themselves in TICKS whether or not they were placed — with a word style each cue adds words, [{start, end, text}] in timeline ticks, and size where a lone word shrank — names the engine that ran, reports under skipped every clip you named that cannot be transcribed, and, whenever replace was given, lists under replaced the tracks it removed. slide, fill and dim each add a look to a word style's pages; whenever one of the three was given, a placed run lists under looks the ones that were on. ENGINE "apple" (the default) is ON-DEVICE speech recognition and nothing leaves this Mac. It needs no grant and has no length ceiling. Its models are installed per Mac: a language whose model is not installed is refused by name and nothing is changed — this tool never downloads one; a person installs it from File ▸ Generate Captions…'s Download row. ENGINE "assemblyai" sends the audio of every media file behind the clips you named — the WHOLE file, not just the parts in use — to AssemblyAI, a cloud service that transcribes it and is billed for it, and the transcript is deleted from AssemblyAI once the words have been read back; do not choose it for somebody else's recording without their say-so. New transcription needs an API key; a nonempty batch whose every media has a current packaged transcript can be reused without a key or network request, unless refresh is true. Pending remote deletion is reported as deferred until a later run with the originating account and region's key can safely retry it. It needs no grant and caches one transcript per media file inside the project package, so a second run over the same files makes no request and reports them under cached. It adds uploaded, cached, audio_seconds (what was billed), speech_model_used, language_detected, endpoint (the host alone of the server a keyed run's requests go to; absent when no API key is set) and, when there is something to say, note. Keyed (spec §11 CJ-3): where it would send to AssemblyAI it needs the person's AssemblyAI API key, and without one it refuses there with "No AssemblyAI API key is set. Set one in Frame Job ▸ Settings… ▸ Transcription.".
