== get_media_analysis
  desc: Answers an analysis of one media file, or of the part of it one clip plays, made ON THIS MAC — no network request, no upload, no API key — and kept per media in the project's cache, so a later call over a window already analysed answers at once. kind "shots" finds the hard cuts: cuts is [{frame, source}] sorted by time, frame the media's frame index and source its presentation time in the media's own SOURCE ticks (600000 per second, of the file played forwards), with count. A cut inside a gradual dissolve is not reported, and a single flash frame is not a cut; a dissolve between flat-coloured pictures (slides, title cards) can show as one cut. kind "masks" finds a person's outline in every frame (Vision's person segmentation, on the Mac) and answers its runs, never pixels: mask_size [w, h] (the masks' own size), frames (frames held in the window), subject_found (whether any frame holds a person: at least 0.5 % of the mask above half), count and runs [{from, to, subject_found, frames}] in SOURCE ticks — a run is consecutive analysed frames that agree on subject_found. Remove Background (Clip ▸ Remove Background, the Clip tab's Picture ▸ Background section, set_video cutout) draws them, and analyze_cutout makes a cut-out clip's masks and says where they stand. kind "faces" finds every face (Vision's face rectangles and landmarks, on the Mac) at 10 samples a second of source time and answers rate, count and samples [{source, frame, faces: [{box: [x, y, w, h], confidence, roll, yaw, mouth_gap, mouth_width}]}]: source the sample's instant in SOURCE ticks, frame the presentation time of the frame it read (the first at or after source, within one sample) or null; box 0…1 of the upright picture from its top-left; roll and yaw in radians; mouth_gap the inner lips' height and mouth_width the outer lips' width, each over the face box's height (gap over width is how open the mouth is). Faces are ordered left to right and carry no identity: follow one by its box from sample to sample. Values are rounded to 4 decimals; null where Vision gave none. A sample row with one face is about 176 bytes (221 with clip), so one answer holds about 296 s of samples (236 s with clip), less when more faces are in frame: read a longer media in from/to windows of at most that, e.g. 2 minutes. kind "sound_events" listens to the media's first sound track (Apple's Sound Analysis classifier, on the Mac) in windows of 900000 ticks (1.5 s) every 450000 (0.75 s) and answers window and hop (ticks), groups [{name, labels, first_class, threshold}] — laughter, applause, cheering, crowd, music, speech, each the classifier labels it is the highest confidence of; none is first_class yet, so read the values as hints, never alone as a finding, and threshold is null — count and windows [{source, laughter, applause, cheering, crowd, music, speech}]: source the window's start in SOURCE ticks and each group's confidence 0…1, rounded to 4 decimals, null where the classifier gave none (a window running past the sound's end). It needs a media with sound, a video or an audio file; a reversed clip is refused, since its sound plays backwards. A row is about 130 bytes, so one answer holds about an hour of windows. kind "subjects" finds people and the regions the eye is drawn to (Vision's upper-body person rectangles and attention-based saliency, on the Mac) at 10 samples a second, each sample instant also a faces sample, and answers rate, count and samples [{source, frame, people: [{box
