== write_graphic
  desc: Writes the JavaScript module graphics/<name>.js in the project's package — atomically, creating graphics/ if it is absent — compiles it, and answers the compile of THAT text: ok, the module's meta and schema, and diagnostics [{level, message, line, column, frame}]. A module that does not compile is an answer, not an error: ok is false and the diagnostics say where; fix the source and write it again. With ok true, diagnostics are the module's top-level warnings (e.g. asset() outside render). clips lists the clips drawn by it, which redraw with the new text at once. A file write is not undoable and does not mark the project edited, but the text it replaced is kept — the last 10 per module; kept lists their revisions, newest first — and restore_graphic brings one back. Read graphic_reference first for the API.
== import_graphic_font
  desc: Copies a .ttf or .otf file into the project's graphics/fonts/ (creating the folders if they are absent), rebuilds the graphics' font set, and answers families — the family names the ENGINE reads from that file, which are the exact strings a font-family must say (they can differ from what other apps show) — and library_families, every family the graphics can now draw with. A file that loads no face is refused in the engine's words. A file of the same name is replaced (replaced is true). Not undoable. Mirrors File ▸ Add Font to Graphics…
== import_graphic_asset
  desc: Copies a PNG, JPEG, GIF, WebP, SVG or SVGZ file into the project's graphics/assets/ (creating the folders if they are absent), where a module draws it with <image href="${asset(name)}">; answers every asset the folder now holds. Anything else, and an empty file, is refused. A file of the same name is replaced (replaced is true). A name asset(name) cannot use (a leading . or #, any of / : " ' < > &, a control character, more than 247 bytes) is filed under a legal one — name says which, renamed_from what was asked; a renamed copy never replaces, it takes -2, -3 and so on. Not undoable. Mirrors File ▸ Add Image to Graphics…
== delete_graphic
  desc: Deletes the module graphics/<name>.js from the project's package. Refused while any clip is drawn by it, on the timeline or inside a nest, in a sentence naming those clips: delete them or point them at another module (set_graphic {name}) first. The deleted text is kept with the module's other kept texts (kept, newest first), so restore_graphic {name} brings the module back; get_graphic {} lists it under history. Fonts and assets are left alone. Not undoable, and it does not mark the project edited. Mirrors File ▸ Delete Graphic…
== rename_graphic
  desc: Renames the module graphics/<name>.js to graphics/<to>.js. Every clip drawn by it, on the timeline and inside every nest, follows the new name as ONE undo step (Rename Graphic; clips lists them), and its kept texts move with it — when to is a deleted module's name (no file, kept texts), the two sets join under to and, past 10, the oldest, the deleted module's, are dropped for good. Undo renames the file back together with the clips, and redo forward again. Refused when to is not a module name, when graphics/<to>.js already exists, when a clip already names to with no file behind it (a clip left by a deleted module), and while a nest is open (set_scope with no arguments comes back out). The file rename itself does not mark the project edited; the clips' change does. Mirrors File ▸ Rename Graphic…
== restore_graphic
  desc: Writes a kept earlier text of a module back as graphics/<name>.js — the newest kept one, or the one revision names — compiles it and answers as write_graphic does (ok, meta, schema, diagnostics, clips), with restored, the revision put back, and kept, what is kept now. The text it replaced is kept in turn, so restoring twice returns to where you were. Works for a module delete_graphic removed. A text is kept whenever the app sees a module's text replaced while the project is open (write_graphic, a save from an outside editor, a restore) and when delete_graphic removes it; the last 10 per module. Not undoable, and it does not mark the project edited. Mirrors File ▸ Restore Graphic Version… (which restores the newest).
== import_graphic
  desc: Copies a .fjgraphic package (one export_graphic or Export… wrote) into the project's graphics/: its module, fonts and assets under the names the manifest gives, as the Import Graphic sheet's Import does. The package is checked first — a stored zip whose entries match its manifest by path, size and SHA-256, a format this app reads, each font loading and each picture a name asset(name) can use — and a refusal writes nothing. A name the project already holds with the same bytes is reused; a module or font name holding something else is suffixed (-2, -3, …; renamed_from says what the package called it); a picture name holding a different picture is refused, since the module reaches it by name. No file is replaced, and a failure part-way removes what was written. Reply: {name (the module), renamed_from?, title, module (written or reused), fonts [{name, renamed_from?, action}], assets 
